//! core/net/server — accepting connections, and writing back on them.
//!
//! `core/net/http` is the client half: `http.send(ctx, request)` makes one
//! request and answers one response. This is the other half — a program that
//! *is* a server rather than one that talks to one — and they are two modules
//! because they are two authorities. `Net` in a context means the program talks
//! to servers; `Listen` means it accepts connections, and `Sockets` means it
//! can push on one somebody else accepted.
//!
//! **The same two types.** `Request` and `Response` are `core/effect`'s, the
//! ones `core/net/http` re-exports: there is one shape for an HTTP message in
//! this language, not one per direction, so a handler here answers with the
//! same `Response` a client reads.
//!
//! **This module is a door, not yet a design.** No platform grants `Listen` or
//! `Sockets` today — `core/host` declares the implementations and the grant
//! table gives them no platform — so nothing here has a caller, and it is
//! deliberately four thin functions rather than a server vocabulary. It exists
//! because an effect method that no standard-library function calls is a method
//! nothing outside `core/*` can perform: an effect is reached by handing the
//! context to a function (SPEC 10.2), and these are those functions. The
//! vocabulary a real server wants — routing, middleware, a `Socket` handle with
//! its own methods, a `CloseReason` instead of a wire code — grows here when a
//! platform grants the effects.

from "core/effect" import { Listen, NetError, Request, Response, Sockets };

from "core/effect" export { NetError, Request, Response };

/// Accepts connections on `address:port`, answers each request with
/// `onRequest`, and returns when the process is asked to shut down.
///
/// The handler is a parameter rather than a capture because an effect-carrying
/// value may be passed only as `self` or `ctx` (SPEC 10.6), so it is *handed*
/// what it needs. What it is handed is this context — `C`, the caller's own —
/// which is what makes a handler able to allocate, print, or read a clock while
/// it answers.
export fn listen<C: Listen>(
    ctx: C,
    address: Str,
    port: Int,
    onRequest: fn(C, Request) => Response,
): Result<(), NetError> {
    ctx.listen(address, port, onRequest)
}

/// Enqueues a text frame on an open socket. Never waits: the frame goes to the
/// socket's outbound buffer, and a socket whose buffer is full is closed rather
/// than its writer blocked.
export fn sendText<C: Sockets>(ctx: C, socket: Int, text: Str): () {
    ctx.socketSendText(socket, text)
}

/// Enqueues a binary frame, on `sendText`'s terms.
export fn sendBytes<C: Sockets>(ctx: C, socket: Int, body: [U8]): () {
    ctx.socketSendBytes(socket, body)
}

/// Closes the socket, with the wire code and reason the far side is told.
///
/// A message sent afterwards is dropped rather than refused: the other end may
/// already be gone, so "did this arrive" was never answerable here.
export fn close<C: Sockets>(ctx: C, socket: Int, code: Int, reason: Str): () {
    ctx.socketClose(socket, code, reason)
}
